### Using the information to solve

In order to find $b'(t_0)$ we need to find $a(t_0)$. Using the Pythagorean theorem and the fact that $b(t_0)=21$ and $c=29$, we can find that $a(t_0)=20$.

Let's plug $\blueD{a(t_0)}=\blueD{20}$, $\maroonD{a'(t_0)}=\maroonD{-7}$, and $\goldD{b(t_0)}=\goldD{21}$ into the expression for $b'(t_0)$:

$\begin{align}
b'(t_0)&=-\dfrac{\blueD{a(t_0)}\maroonD{a'(t_0)}}{\goldD{b(t_0)}}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{(\blueD{20})(\maroonD{-7})}{(\goldD{21})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{20}{3}
\end{align}$
